=== Prompt ===
Who is Queen Elizabeth II?

=== Response ===
Queen Elizabeth II was the monarch of the United Kingdom from February 6, 1952, until her death on September 8, 2022. She was the longest-reigning British monarch in history, serving for over 70 years.

Born on April 21, 1926, Elizabeth Alexandra Mary Windsor was the first child of Prince Albert, Duke of York (later King George VI), and his wife, Elizabeth Bowes-Lyon (later Queen Elizabeth The Queen Mother). She was educated at home and received military training during World War II.

In 1947, Elizabeth married Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh, a former prince of Greece and Denmark. The couple had four children: Charles, Prince of Wales; Anne, Princess Royal; Prince Andrew, Duke of York; and Prince Edward, Earl of Wessex.

Elizabeth became queen at the age of 25 after her father's death in 1952. During her reign, she played a crucial role in promoting British interests and supporting the Commonwealth, a voluntary association of 56 countries, most of which were formerly part of the British Empire.

Throughout her life, Queen Elizabeth II was known for her dedication to duty, her sense of humor, and her commitment to public service. She carried out numerous engagements and ceremonies, including the annual Trooping the Colour ceremony, the State Opening of Parliament, and the annual Christmas message.

Queen Elizabeth II was also a patron of numerous organizations and charities, including the British Red Cross, the Scouts, and the Commonwealth Games Federation. She was also a strong supporter of the arts, and her patronage included the Royal Shakespeare Company and the Royal Opera House.

In 2012, Queen Elizabeth II celebrated her Diamond Jubilee, marking 60 years on the throne. She also celebrated her Platinum Jubilee in 2022, marking 70 years on the throne, but sadly passed away on September 8, 2022, at the age of 96.

Queen Elizabeth II's legacy is one of service, duty, and dedication to her country and the Commonwealth. She will be remembered as one of the most iconic and enduring figures of the 20th and 21st centuries.

